1
00:00:08,266 --> 00:00:09,100
[car starting]

2
00:00:09,166 --> 00:00:10,000
[engine revving]

3
00:00:13,513 --> 00:00:15,648
[upbeat music playing]

4
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:20,400
[Jerry] This is a 1995
Volvo 960 station wagon.

5
00:00:20,453 --> 00:00:21,888
Oh, my God. Really?

6
00:00:24,933 --> 00:00:26,033
It is in regent red,

7
00:00:26,099 --> 00:00:29,933
it has a black leather interior,
premium sound system and a sunroof.

8
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:30,966
But...

9
00:00:32,599 --> 00:00:35,168
it was built with
a high-performance racing engine

10
00:00:35,233 --> 00:00:37,166
by none other than Paul Newman.

11
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:40,933
It's a 380-horsepower Ford V8
with a supercharger

12
00:00:41,041 --> 00:00:43,143
and a Mustang five-speed gearbox.

13
00:00:44,633 --> 00:00:47,800
It looks like the kind of car
that you would take to a place like this.

14
00:00:47,866 --> 00:00:49,166
And do things like this.

15
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,766
But it's actually a car
where you can do things like this.

16
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:55,133
[tires screeching]

17
00:00:58,091 --> 00:00:59,325
[music pitches down and stops]

18
00:00:59,866 --> 00:01:00,766
[brakes screech]

19
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,333
[tires screeching]

20
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:08,601
[music resumes]

21
00:01:11,866 --> 00:01:12,833
[phone ringing]

22
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,366
-[David] Hello?
-[Jerry] Hello, Dave? It's Jerry.

23
00:01:15,442 --> 00:01:16,843
-[David] Jerry...
-[Jerry] Seinfeld.

24
00:01:16,933 --> 00:01:17,766
[David] Oh, yeah.

25
00:01:17,877 --> 00:01:19,612
[Jerry] I was kind of
in the mood for coffee.

26
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:21,500
-Are you around?
-[David] Let's go.

27
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:31,033
[Jerry] Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and
this is Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

28
00:01:31,566 --> 00:01:34,566
Today, my guest is talk show legend
David Letterman.

29
00:01:34,866 --> 00:01:38,166
This Volvo is actually his car,
but he's letting me drive it today.

30
00:01:38,399 --> 00:01:39,233
[dinging]

31
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:41,100
May I have two men, please?

32
00:01:41,233 --> 00:01:44,000
-Is gay marriage legal in Connecticut?
-I don't believe so.

33
00:01:45,038 --> 00:01:46,439
[engine revving]

34
00:01:49,966 --> 00:01:52,766
Hi, Jerry, how are you? Nice to see you.

35
00:01:53,133 --> 00:01:54,399
We're going for coffee.

36
00:01:54,899 --> 00:01:56,066
Just wanted to let Mom know.

37
00:01:56,149 --> 00:01:57,951
[jazz piano playing]

38
00:01:58,033 --> 00:02:00,699
[David] I just love the car
because of Paul Newman.

39
00:02:00,866 --> 00:02:05,566
He called me one day and he said, "A guy
is building me a Volvo station wagon

40
00:02:05,633 --> 00:02:07,966
with a racing engine. Would you like one?"

41
00:02:08,366 --> 00:02:11,900
And because it was Paul Newman,
you know, I was a little intimidated.

42
00:02:11,966 --> 00:02:15,266
And I said, "Yes, I do, Paul.
I'd like one of those cars."

43
00:02:15,368 --> 00:02:17,370
And he said,
"Great. Do you want a puffer on it?"

44
00:02:17,466 --> 00:02:18,966
So I said, "Sure. Sure."

45
00:02:19,033 --> 00:02:22,466
And I got off the phone and I said,
"Anybody know what a puffer is?"

46
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:24,711
-[Jerry chuckles]
-[David] And it's a supercharger.

47
00:02:24,811 --> 00:02:27,280
-[Jerry] His sense of humor, isn't it?
-[David] Yes.

48
00:02:28,433 --> 00:02:30,933
-[David] What is this?
-[Jerry] Well, Dave, it's a family car.

49
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,933
So today, this is the family.
That's me and that's you.

50
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:37,366
One day I had a cardiac appointment.
This was before my surgery.

51
00:02:37,733 --> 00:02:42,066
So I said, "Great, we'll take the Volvo
into the city." [laughs]

52
00:02:42,433 --> 00:02:45,066
And the car catches fire
on the Merritt Parkway.

53
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:46,033
[both chuckling]

54
00:02:46,166 --> 00:02:51,366
The tailpipe coming off the manifold,
cherry red. Glowing.

55
00:02:51,638 --> 00:02:52,739
Coulda killed me, I guess.

56
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:53,866
[both chuckling]

57
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:57,076
[Jerry] The hell with the cardiologist,
you were already in flames.

58
00:02:57,143 --> 00:02:59,112
-[engine humming]
-[upbeat jazz playing]

59
00:03:00,933 --> 00:03:04,733
[David] This is a treat for me.
You're driving the Paul Newman Volvo.

60
00:03:04,799 --> 00:03:06,166
This is fantastic.

61
00:03:06,386 --> 00:03:09,255
-[thuds]
-[grunts] Had it lowered a little.

62
00:03:09,333 --> 00:03:10,233
[bell dings]

63
00:03:12,466 --> 00:03:13,799
-What is that?
-Want kettle corn?

64
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,200
-How did you know I like kettle corn?
-I had a feeling.

65
00:03:17,633 --> 00:03:20,633
[Jerry] So I took it to Stew Leonard's.
Have you been there?

66
00:03:20,833 --> 00:03:24,166
[David] I went once, and I was
so frightened that I never went back.

67
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:28,166
Somewhere near "dairy,"
I abandoned the cart and ran out.

68
00:03:28,299 --> 00:03:29,500
Is that your experience?

69
00:03:29,576 --> 00:03:31,911
-Yeah, same thing happened to me.
-[laughs]

70
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:35,700
[Jerry] There was one guy
who had the trifecta.

71
00:03:35,766 --> 00:03:39,466
He was hugely fat
pushing a giant load of food

72
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:41,566
-and chewing at the same time.
-Yeah.

73
00:03:41,866 --> 00:03:45,166
He bought something probably
at the deli counter just to hold him over.

74
00:03:45,233 --> 00:03:46,933
Yeah. Right.

75
00:03:47,133 --> 00:03:49,333
Speaking of dumb guys,
how about this fella up here?

76
00:03:49,399 --> 00:03:53,166
Yeah. "Speaking of dumb guys,"
always a segue.

77
00:03:55,033 --> 00:03:57,500
[Jerry] This is a sensationally
well-done car.

78
00:03:57,566 --> 00:03:59,066
[David] Not sure how well-done it is.

79
00:03:59,133 --> 00:04:03,500
I remember taking my father-in-law out
in the car and then it just quit.

80
00:04:03,833 --> 00:04:08,466
You ever been ten miles from your house
in a dead car with your father-in-law?

81
00:04:08,633 --> 00:04:10,266
-No.
-That's a full day.

82
00:04:10,799 --> 00:04:14,533
I mean, he's a wonderful man,
but that's a full day.

83
00:04:15,733 --> 00:04:16,766
[Jerry] In the mood for coffee?

84
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,700
[David] I love coffee. Honest to God,
I can't get enough of it.

85
00:04:19,933 --> 00:04:23,666
Now, can you get to a place where,
just for me, you can tap it a little bit?

86
00:04:23,766 --> 00:04:24,666
Watch this.

87
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,099
[engine revving]

88
00:04:30,266 --> 00:04:31,566
[David laughing]

89
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:35,000
Well, that's nice.
That's so nice, isn't it?

90
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:37,233
-Yeah.
-I mean, come on.

91
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:40,866
God bless Paul.
That did my heart good. Wow!

92
00:04:41,166 --> 00:04:42,733
But, honest to God, it does smell

93
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:45,700
like we're seconds away
from incinerating something.

94
00:04:45,866 --> 00:04:47,266
My feet are getting hot.

95
00:04:47,466 --> 00:04:51,000
-[Jerry] It's just toasty in here.
-[David] The kettle corn is popping again.

96
00:04:53,066 --> 00:04:55,500
[Jerry] You remember
The Comedy Store in the '70s?

97
00:04:55,566 --> 00:04:56,700
Pryor would come in.

98
00:04:56,766 --> 00:05:00,266
I had the, uh, the great fun
to follow him one night.

99
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:04,800
Because The Comedy Store, after Richard,
did everything but explode.

100
00:05:04,866 --> 00:05:07,066
People are laughing so hard.

101
00:05:07,133 --> 00:05:09,366
"And now, ladies and gentlemen,
Dave Letterman."

102
00:05:09,566 --> 00:05:13,700
And of course, I have to fight people
who are leaving to get to the stage.

103
00:05:13,833 --> 00:05:17,633
I kind of got friendly with Richard
and really thought he was a dear guy.

104
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:20,300
He closed with a bit about
having sex with a dog.

105
00:05:20,466 --> 00:05:22,800
And I just thought, "Well, okay.

106
00:05:23,433 --> 00:05:25,666
There are many facets of genius."

107
00:05:26,133 --> 00:05:27,066
Yes, there are.

108
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:30,966
[David] Colin Quinn, I read a thing

109
00:05:31,033 --> 00:05:33,300
in the Times where
he's like a big Twitter guy.

110
00:05:33,366 --> 00:05:34,200
[Jerry] Big Twitter guy.

111
00:05:34,266 --> 00:05:36,133
Your wife was quoted in the article.

112
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,066
She was? Yeah, maybe she was.

113
00:05:38,333 --> 00:05:39,500
Everything all right at home?

114
00:05:39,566 --> 00:05:41,233
[both laughing]

115
00:05:42,942 --> 00:05:44,143
[Jerry] Remember in the '90s,

116
00:05:44,233 --> 00:05:46,533
you were in LA,
and you came on the set of my show?

117
00:05:46,733 --> 00:05:47,566
[audience laughing]

118
00:05:48,333 --> 00:05:49,166
[audience cheering]

119
00:05:49,233 --> 00:05:50,900
Oh, no, it's my weird cousin Larry.

120
00:05:50,966 --> 00:05:52,966
-What are you guys talking about?
-Sandwiches.

121
00:05:53,066 --> 00:05:55,533
Oh, man, don't get me started
on sandwiches.

122
00:05:56,666 --> 00:05:57,766
[Jerry] That was so weird.

123
00:05:58,599 --> 00:06:00,599
What do you think of my driving so far?

124
00:06:00,666 --> 00:06:02,500
This'll be the real test,
the parallel parking.

125
00:06:02,566 --> 00:06:03,533
Yeah. Well, watch this.

126
00:06:04,833 --> 00:06:08,233
Oh, my! Ladies and gentlemen,
now that he's got his helmet off,

127
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:09,766
let's really let him hear it.

128
00:06:10,066 --> 00:06:12,533
-Wow. That was impressive.
-That's what they say at Indy?

129
00:06:12,599 --> 00:06:14,366
-Yeah.
-Now that he's got his helmet off...

130
00:06:14,407 --> 00:06:15,808
Yeah. Let's really let him hear it.

131
00:06:15,875 --> 00:06:18,111
-[Jerry] Do you want any more kettle corn?
-No, thanks.

132
00:06:18,833 --> 00:06:20,700
Come over,
I want you to look at something.

133
00:06:21,299 --> 00:06:22,400
-I mean...
-How you doing?

134
00:06:22,466 --> 00:06:24,033
-Good, Randy, how are you?
-You're here.

135
00:06:24,133 --> 00:06:26,633
-I mean, if we were robbing a bank...
-Hey, Bill. Hey, Randy.

136
00:06:26,799 --> 00:06:27,966
-Bill. Randy.
-[Randy] Hey.

137
00:06:28,020 --> 00:06:31,023
-[upbeat jazz piano playing]
-[no audible dialogue]

138
00:06:36,195 --> 00:06:38,398
-[Jerry] I'd like some coffee. Yeah.
-[waitress] Sure.

139
00:06:38,466 --> 00:06:39,933
I'd like some coffee as well, please.

140
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:41,833
[hissing]

141
00:06:43,369 --> 00:06:44,971
[Jerry] What time did you get up
this morning?

142
00:06:45,066 --> 00:06:46,066
[David] I got up at 7:00.

143
00:06:46,233 --> 00:06:49,099
First, we take the dog out,
and it's, uh, Frisbee.

144
00:06:49,333 --> 00:06:51,299
Then it's shovel time.

145
00:06:51,433 --> 00:06:53,533
-You have to get up with your dog?
-[Jerry] No.

146
00:06:53,733 --> 00:06:56,900
In the apartment we have... There's
a little paper thing, they go on that.

147
00:06:57,333 --> 00:06:59,400
Has the Board of Health
signed off on that?

148
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:01,333
[Jerry] Yeah, they gave us a C.

149
00:07:05,866 --> 00:07:08,666
Not drinking coffee if you're not
drinking it black. Don't you think?

150
00:07:09,133 --> 00:07:11,599
-No, I don't think.
-You like to monkey with it?

151
00:07:11,866 --> 00:07:12,700
[laughs]

152
00:07:13,033 --> 00:07:15,500
Now I'm afraid to talk
because of these people.

153
00:07:15,566 --> 00:07:16,666
Or are these actors?

154
00:07:16,733 --> 00:07:18,266
[Jerry] No, they're people, Dave.

155
00:07:18,566 --> 00:07:20,299
Can we just ask these people to leave?

156
00:07:20,366 --> 00:07:21,633
We don't own this place.

157
00:07:21,866 --> 00:07:22,966
[both laughing]

158
00:07:23,533 --> 00:07:25,299
We could change that though, can't we?

159
00:07:30,033 --> 00:07:34,433
You know, you've had so many fantastic
lines that you've done on your show.

160
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,866
Do those rattle around your head ever
or are they all gone?

161
00:07:36,956 --> 00:07:38,191
Sometimes somebody will,

162
00:07:38,799 --> 00:07:41,633
"Remember the time..." and I'll say,
"Really, I said that?"

163
00:07:41,833 --> 00:07:45,500
Do you remember the woman that said,
"Have you ever had the hump of a camel?"

164
00:07:45,566 --> 00:07:50,066
I had a, um... Have you ever had
the hump of a camel?

165
00:07:50,166 --> 00:07:51,500
[audience laughing]

166
00:07:53,105 --> 00:07:55,641
-Yeah, I do remember that.
-Yeah. And there was that long beat.

167
00:07:55,733 --> 00:07:58,466
And I wondered if you had it right away
or you were...

168
00:07:58,533 --> 00:08:00,666
Or it came as... In that laugh.

169
00:08:00,733 --> 00:08:03,466
What I do remember, "Is the double meaning

170
00:08:03,533 --> 00:08:07,433
that I'm leveraging this joke on
gonna hold up?"

171
00:08:07,833 --> 00:08:11,633
No, I've, uh... I've never had
the hump of a camel.

172
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:13,333
-[audience laughing]
-I didn't think so.

173
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:17,866
But I must say, when I was younger,
I was pretty good.

174
00:08:17,933 --> 00:08:19,000
[audience laughing]

175
00:08:19,833 --> 00:08:20,666
So you have?

176
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:21,900
[audience cheering]

177
00:08:22,666 --> 00:08:24,933
-[David] I knew it was a setup.
-Right.

178
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,066
But I didn't know that "hump" would be
the fulcrum, you know?

179
00:08:28,299 --> 00:08:32,600
Yeah, and you think, "Gee, I don't wanna
waste this fat pitch on something."

180
00:08:32,666 --> 00:08:34,400
But it really was so great.

181
00:08:34,466 --> 00:08:37,133
-But I'll tell you, often, somebody...
-Right.

182
00:08:37,183 --> 00:08:38,651
-Thank you.
-[waitress] You're welcome.

183
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:39,866
[David] Sure.

184
00:08:45,866 --> 00:08:47,766
Something from the lab, apparently.

185
00:08:48,033 --> 00:08:48,900
[laughing] Yes.

186
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:52,533
Have you found football
any harder to watch?

187
00:08:52,666 --> 00:08:55,900
I think they should get rid of time-outs.
They should get rid of punts.

188
00:08:56,066 --> 00:08:56,900
And also,

189
00:08:56,966 --> 00:08:59,633
I think they should be allowed to play
as many guys as they'd like.

190
00:09:00,266 --> 00:09:02,699
If you got a play for 40 guys,
send them in.

191
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:06,400
Your team, uh, gets
three successive first downs.

192
00:09:06,812 --> 00:09:09,982
They then have the option
of running a play with two balls.

193
00:09:10,049 --> 00:09:11,484
Feel like I'm in Gallagher's act now.

194
00:09:11,733 --> 00:09:12,966
[both laughing]

195
00:09:15,087 --> 00:09:16,455
That is a little "Gallagher."

196
00:09:22,033 --> 00:09:24,333
Here's something I'm good at,
going to the hardware store.

197
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:25,933
-I love going to the hardware store.
-Yeah.

198
00:09:26,032 --> 00:09:29,135
And my son, we were in
the hardware store one day and he says...

199
00:09:29,233 --> 00:09:30,066
[sniffing]

200
00:09:30,133 --> 00:09:31,699
"I love how it smells here." I said...

201
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,633
-Smells great. Smells great.
-Smells fantastic. Yeah.

202
00:09:34,866 --> 00:09:36,533
Over the winter
we built a little push car.

203
00:09:36,833 --> 00:09:40,533
So he goes down the driveway,
loses control and goes over like this.

204
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:42,666
But it was all pretty minor.

205
00:09:42,866 --> 00:09:45,766
Two hours later,
his first baseball practice of the season.

206
00:09:46,266 --> 00:09:50,166
So now he says, "I can't go to baseball
practice because I've hurt my hand."

207
00:09:50,423 --> 00:09:53,359
-Right.
-So, what do you do in that situation?

208
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:56,100
I support whatever position my wife takes.

209
00:09:56,533 --> 00:09:58,100
-[laughing]
-That's what I do.

210
00:09:58,333 --> 00:10:00,733
Because she knows more
about parenting than you?

211
00:10:00,833 --> 00:10:02,366
The answer: Doesn't matter what you do.

212
00:10:02,435 --> 00:10:04,904
-But why have a fight with your wife?
-The... [chuckles]

213
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,133
Yeah. But the worst part of that is...

214
00:10:07,199 --> 00:10:10,666
Maybe you don't experience this
since you mentioned that you don't care.

215
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:12,433
[both laughing]

216
00:10:13,366 --> 00:10:16,433
Is that you feel guilty
about your own behavior.

217
00:10:16,566 --> 00:10:18,733
You know, I kind of look at my family now
and I think,

218
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:21,366
"Well, in 60 years everyone's dead here."

219
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:23,766
So... [laughing]

220
00:10:25,566 --> 00:10:26,833
Could I have the check, please?

221
00:10:28,199 --> 00:10:29,733
Good Lord!

222
00:10:30,033 --> 00:10:32,233
-Oh, my goodness!
-It's just that...

223
00:10:32,533 --> 00:10:36,699
I just don't like this over-consideration
of every decision that's...

224
00:10:36,766 --> 00:10:37,600
Well, that's me.

225
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,699
If I had more than one,

226
00:10:39,766 --> 00:10:42,633
I'd feel a little more comfortable about
the decisions

227
00:10:42,699 --> 00:10:44,333
I would make with either one.

228
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:46,466
You've used that excuse a number of times.
I don't buy it.

229
00:10:46,533 --> 00:10:49,600
You're gonna react to two the same as one.
A kid is a kid.

230
00:10:49,666 --> 00:10:51,199
-That's what I've heard.
-Kid's a kid.

231
00:10:51,266 --> 00:10:53,066
I agree that being an older parent,

232
00:10:53,199 --> 00:10:56,433
a lot of the real serious stuff,
I'll be dead for.

233
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,033
You don't know that.

234
00:10:58,566 --> 00:11:01,699
I don't know that,
but sometimes it's useful.

235
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:02,733
[laughing]

236
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:05,666
What if he told you
he wanted to be a comedian?

237
00:11:05,833 --> 00:11:07,033
I don't think that'll come up.

238
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:10,400
His stepfather, Scott, will...
will deal with that.

239
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:13,000
Scott will get him into a hedge fund.

240
00:11:13,566 --> 00:11:14,866
[both laughing]

241
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:20,966
The comedy universe is a swamp of madness.

242
00:11:21,033 --> 00:11:23,500
-Yes.
-Haven't you enjoyed in your life,

243
00:11:23,633 --> 00:11:26,666
that you've been really surrounded by it?

244
00:11:26,966 --> 00:11:28,033
The Comedy Store.

245
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:32,166
I mean, it was one after another
of guys who were really funny

246
00:11:32,233 --> 00:11:35,000
-and guys who had personality disorders.
-Yeah.

247
00:11:35,133 --> 00:11:37,100
[David] Lenny Schultz comes to mind.

248
00:11:37,166 --> 00:11:38,933
-[Jerry] You saw him?
-[David] Oh, my God.

249
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,166
We talk about Lenny Schultz
once a week now.

250
00:11:41,433 --> 00:11:44,533
I remember one time
Lenny's got a BabyBjorn thing.

251
00:11:44,733 --> 00:11:48,600
And he's running around onstage with the
plastic doll and he has a little toy whip.

252
00:11:48,708 --> 00:11:50,209
-Right.
-And he's whipping the baby.

253
00:11:50,333 --> 00:11:53,433
Because you hear the sound effects
of a crying baby.

254
00:11:53,766 --> 00:11:57,566
And then, Lenny is whipping the baby.
"Be quiet. William Morris is here.

255
00:11:57,633 --> 00:12:01,366
Stop it. William Morris is
looking at my act. Stop it, baby."

256
00:12:01,433 --> 00:12:04,000
And he's whipping the plastic doll.

257
00:12:04,366 --> 00:12:07,166
Because William Morris
is taking a look at it.

258
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:09,199
It was delightful.

259
00:12:09,566 --> 00:12:11,600
And where did you go on
in the very beginning?

260
00:12:11,766 --> 00:12:13,766
-Comedy Store. Yeah.
-First time onstage?

261
00:12:13,933 --> 00:12:15,600
-Did you get any laughs?
-No.

262
00:12:15,666 --> 00:12:16,500
-Nothing?
-No. No.

263
00:12:16,833 --> 00:12:18,900
-I remember being shocked by the quiet.
-Mm-hmm.

264
00:12:18,966 --> 00:12:20,933
'Cause I had watched
a lot of comedians and thought,

265
00:12:21,066 --> 00:12:23,366
"Well, the audience seems to be
laughing anyway."

266
00:12:23,433 --> 00:12:26,233
And then when you go up there,
you find out they're not laughing at all.

267
00:12:26,433 --> 00:12:29,933
I had to go to a place in Denver, uh,
called the Turn of the Century.

268
00:12:30,133 --> 00:12:33,333
And I was opening for Leslie Uggams.

269
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:34,233
Wow!

270
00:12:34,533 --> 00:12:36,633
And you're supposed to do 45 minutes.

271
00:12:36,900 --> 00:12:38,066
-To open?
-To open.

272
00:12:38,133 --> 00:12:40,400
-Well, I had about 35.
-[chuckles] Right.

273
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:42,566
And the guarantee is
they were gonna turn the house.

274
00:12:42,833 --> 00:12:44,666
-Right.
-They didn't turn the house.

275
00:12:46,233 --> 00:12:49,966
So, the second show, it's,
"Hi, where you from?"

276
00:12:50,333 --> 00:12:52,000
And the guy says, "I'm from Denver."

277
00:12:52,066 --> 00:12:53,933
And I said, "Ah. Great, Denver."

278
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,099
And I said,
"Where are you from?" "Denver."

279
00:12:56,366 --> 00:13:00,533
And the guy says, "Look,
we're all from Denver. You're in Denver."

280
00:13:01,166 --> 00:13:04,466
I said, "Thank you, good night.
And now Leslie Uggams."

281
00:13:04,533 --> 00:13:05,366
[both laughing]

282
00:13:09,355 --> 00:13:10,589
You guys want something?

283
00:13:10,666 --> 00:13:11,900
[David] I'm really not hungry.

284
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,866
-But get something for yourself.
-Okay. [laughs]

285
00:13:13,933 --> 00:13:15,800
Leonard used to say that wherever we'd go.

286
00:13:16,099 --> 00:13:17,133
[imitating Leonard] "Not hungry,

287
00:13:17,199 --> 00:13:18,800
-but get something for yourself."
-[waitress laughs]

288
00:13:18,900 --> 00:13:19,766
Ever eaten with him?

289
00:13:19,833 --> 00:13:23,533
I... I remember, uh, years ago he was
living with a woman named Adele.

290
00:13:23,602 --> 00:13:25,771
He called up and says:
[imitating Leonard] "Letterman,

291
00:13:25,866 --> 00:13:28,266
why don't you come over
and we'll write some jokes and stuff."

292
00:13:28,599 --> 00:13:29,900
I said, "Great,"

293
00:13:30,066 --> 00:13:33,666
knowing full well that
there's nothing I could contribute to Jay.

294
00:13:33,933 --> 00:13:36,500
-Right.
-And I go over there and it's noontime,

295
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:40,133
and Adele brings him out,
what looked like an enormous,

296
00:13:40,233 --> 00:13:41,900
I don't know, porterhouse.

297
00:13:41,966 --> 00:13:45,233
I don't know,
a big fallen-over-the-platter

298
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:46,633
-piece of steak.
-Right.

299
00:13:47,099 --> 00:13:48,466
He... He gets right into it.

300
00:13:48,566 --> 00:13:49,866
And then she comes out

301
00:13:50,099 --> 00:13:53,166
with a, uh, carton of milk,
a gallon carton of milk.

302
00:13:53,533 --> 00:13:55,199
[chuckles] And he's drinking the milk,

303
00:13:55,500 --> 00:13:58,633
and his face is now
glistening with meat grease.

304
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:01,400
And there was never any question of:

305
00:14:01,466 --> 00:14:03,066
[imitating Leonard]
"Can I get you something to eat?"

306
00:14:04,166 --> 00:14:05,000
Never happened.

307
00:14:05,699 --> 00:14:07,599
Yeah, the few times I've watched him eat,

308
00:14:07,666 --> 00:14:11,333
my only thought is, "Let me just
not get my hand caught in there."

309
00:14:11,433 --> 00:14:12,500
It's like a combine.

310
00:14:14,653 --> 00:14:17,990
One night we had Rickles on,
and we had Regis on,

311
00:14:18,166 --> 00:14:19,666
and we had Tony Bennett on.

312
00:14:19,933 --> 00:14:21,466
-The same show?
-On the same show.

313
00:14:21,599 --> 00:14:23,800
-It was for the kids.
-[Jerry chuckles]

314
00:14:23,966 --> 00:14:28,633
But that, to me, was reminiscent
of Johnny in Burbank.

315
00:14:28,933 --> 00:14:31,066
Did Johnny talk to you during commercials?

316
00:14:31,233 --> 00:14:34,099
He would sometimes
give me stuff about bits.

317
00:14:34,733 --> 00:14:36,566
He would say,
"Why don't you try this or that?"

318
00:14:36,633 --> 00:14:39,099
-He did that to a lot of guys.
-What good is it now?

319
00:14:39,166 --> 00:14:40,466
I've already burned it here.

320
00:14:40,633 --> 00:14:42,199
[both laughing]

321
00:14:43,466 --> 00:14:46,666
When in your timeline
did you start guesting on that show?

322
00:14:46,966 --> 00:14:48,533
May 7th, 1981.

323
00:14:48,666 --> 00:14:50,133
So, the pressure of--

324
00:14:50,189 --> 00:14:52,524
-Either I have a career or I don't.
-Right. Exactly right.

325
00:14:52,599 --> 00:14:53,500
-Right?
-Yep.

326
00:14:53,666 --> 00:14:56,633
Like, I either make it or break it,
right here, right now.

327
00:14:56,699 --> 00:14:58,500
-The guy pulls the curtain open...
-Yeah.

328
00:14:58,633 --> 00:15:01,433
-...and the rest of your life is there.
-Yeah.

329
00:15:01,633 --> 00:15:04,733
Jonathan Winters dropped dead
the other day. That's just God-awful.

330
00:15:05,033 --> 00:15:07,300
-Did you love Jonathan Winters?
-Absolutely.

331
00:15:07,366 --> 00:15:08,199
Adored him.

332
00:15:08,266 --> 00:15:10,366
He would come out on The Ed Sullivan Show

333
00:15:10,433 --> 00:15:13,400
and I would start laughing
when he came out.

334
00:15:13,466 --> 00:15:17,533
Yes. I don't think comedy has changed
and it ever will change.

335
00:15:17,833 --> 00:15:19,533
What about the mechanism of comedy?

336
00:15:19,599 --> 00:15:20,566
No. No change.

337
00:15:20,633 --> 00:15:22,133
-No change?
-No change.

338
00:15:22,199 --> 00:15:26,800
You don't care, uh, if a guy's doing
his "having sex with a dog" bit.

339
00:15:26,966 --> 00:15:29,099
As long as they don't make me do it,
I don't care.

340
00:15:29,166 --> 00:15:32,199
It's justice.
Comedy is the closest thing to justice.

341
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:35,666
If you're funny, you survive.
If you're not, you don't.

342
00:15:35,733 --> 00:15:37,566
Lenny Schultz, God bless him,

343
00:15:37,633 --> 00:15:39,500
you know, he would be doing great now.

344
00:15:39,666 --> 00:15:42,833
I can remember for a time
he had a little person in his act.

345
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:46,199
And his name was Corky.
And he would invite Corky...

346
00:15:48,666 --> 00:15:51,733
up onstage to sit on his lap and...

347
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:53,300
[chuckling]

348
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:55,133
We'll just let it go at that.

349
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:56,733
You be the judge. Is that--

350
00:15:56,800 --> 00:16:00,099
Do you think there was a meeting
of little people to decide,

351
00:16:00,233 --> 00:16:01,833
"Well, what should we be called?"

352
00:16:02,099 --> 00:16:03,900
Do you think they sat around and said,

353
00:16:03,966 --> 00:16:05,766
"Look, we gotta
get rid of this midget thing.

354
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:09,366
We need another term of our own choosing."

355
00:16:09,666 --> 00:16:11,699
Do you think such a meeting
ever took place?

356
00:16:11,966 --> 00:16:13,599
Yes, I... I believe that there was.

357
00:16:13,733 --> 00:16:15,366
And what do you think some of the other...

358
00:16:15,933 --> 00:16:17,000
I don't know.

359
00:16:17,199 --> 00:16:19,733
Do you think "fun-size"
came into the conversation?

360
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:22,366
I knew you were working on material.

361
00:16:22,733 --> 00:16:24,133
[both laughing]

362
00:16:25,699 --> 00:16:27,666
Do you find yourself
always trying to be funny

363
00:16:27,733 --> 00:16:30,633
and sometimes people
just don't wanna be bothered or...

364
00:16:30,699 --> 00:16:32,000
Yeah, pretty regularly.

365
00:16:32,099 --> 00:16:33,733
-Regularly?
-Yeah.

366
00:16:33,866 --> 00:16:37,233
Now, is this about how long
you talk to your guests on this show?

367
00:16:37,300 --> 00:16:38,400
No, this is longer.

368
00:16:38,466 --> 00:16:39,666
Why is that?

369
00:16:39,733 --> 00:16:41,866
-I think we're getting on.
-Oh. Oh, okay.

370
00:16:41,933 --> 00:16:44,733
I thought maybe you were still waiting
for something good to happen.

371
00:16:45,033 --> 00:16:45,866
Oh, no.

372
00:16:46,300 --> 00:16:49,099
[laughing] You gave...
You gave up on that.

373
00:16:50,300 --> 00:16:51,433
Let's get out of here.

374
00:16:54,813 --> 00:16:56,281
Wanna check out that hardware store?

375
00:16:56,699 --> 00:16:57,566
[David] Mm-hmm.

376
00:16:57,633 --> 00:16:59,933
-[Jerry] Let's see if it has the smell.
-[David] It will.

377
00:17:00,066 --> 00:17:01,033
[David laughing]

378
00:17:01,133 --> 00:17:02,500
There it is. [sniffing]

379
00:17:02,599 --> 00:17:04,200
-That's it.
-What is that?

380
00:17:04,366 --> 00:17:06,066
I don't know, it's fantastic.

381
00:17:06,266 --> 00:17:07,466
Is someone helping you?

382
00:17:07,666 --> 00:17:09,766
-Are you looking for a grill? Oh.
-No.

383
00:17:09,828 --> 00:17:12,064
Did we just pin you in
and you're trying to get out?

384
00:17:12,131 --> 00:17:13,766
-Yes.
-[David] She's being held hostage. Run.

385
00:17:13,832 --> 00:17:15,401
Run for your life, ma'am.

386
00:17:15,467 --> 00:17:17,469
-[Jerry] Bye, thank you.
-[woman] Have a great day.

387
00:17:18,366 --> 00:17:20,433
-Now here's where the car breaks down...
-[laughing]

388
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:22,333
...and we're stuck here
for the rest of the night.

389
00:17:22,766 --> 00:17:25,000
-Do you have the keys, Jerry?
-Yeah, I got them.

390
00:17:27,546 --> 00:17:29,214
[Jerry] Could we try driving
one of your other cars?

391
00:17:29,633 --> 00:17:30,733
[David] Can I pick the car?

392
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:31,733
-[Jerry] Sure.
-[David] Okay.

393
00:17:33,752 --> 00:17:35,554
-[metal grinding]
-[David groans]

394
00:17:37,089 --> 00:17:38,490
[David] Thanks for that kettle corn.

395
00:17:38,733 --> 00:17:41,433
In addition to being delicious,
it was hilarious.

396
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:43,433
[both laughing]

397
00:17:43,833 --> 00:17:44,833
Thank you.

398
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:46,833
Oh, no, not this.

399
00:17:46,966 --> 00:17:47,833
[laughing]

400
00:17:47,899 --> 00:17:49,433
-[Jerry] No, I won't.
-[David] Yeah.

401
00:17:49,633 --> 00:17:51,733
Oh, God, I don't wanna be in this.

402
00:17:52,033 --> 00:17:54,766
[David] First time I drove it,
it was so quiet, I ran over a squirrel.

403
00:17:57,466 --> 00:18:00,899
-You really don't like riding in this car.
-[Jerry] No.

404
00:18:01,133 --> 00:18:04,466
[David] You just wanna burn up
as much fossil fuel as you can.

405
00:18:04,533 --> 00:18:05,866
[Jerry] It's like alcohol, Dave.

406
00:18:06,066 --> 00:18:08,666
We have a problem with this stuff.
Let's finish it up.

407
00:18:08,733 --> 00:18:10,500
[David laughing]

408
00:18:10,899 --> 00:18:13,933
[David] People just make fun of me
when they see me in this car.

409
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,200
-And I feel like a putz, you know?
-[Jerry] Right.

410
00:18:16,266 --> 00:18:17,800
Do you know what a putz is?

411
00:18:17,866 --> 00:18:19,300
[David] Well, I... Yeah.

412
00:18:19,366 --> 00:18:21,233
-[Jerry] Know the word "schmuck"?
-[David] Yeah.

413
00:18:21,433 --> 00:18:22,800
[Jerry] So, what's the difference?

414
00:18:22,866 --> 00:18:25,233
[David] A schmuck would be a guy
riding around with a putz

415
00:18:25,300 --> 00:18:26,566
while he's eating kettle corn.

416
00:18:26,633 --> 00:18:27,566
[Jerry chuckling]

417
00:18:27,633 --> 00:18:29,200
Is that how that breaks out?

418
00:18:29,800 --> 00:18:30,766
[David chuckling]

419
00:18:30,899 --> 00:18:33,300
I'm driving till the red light
comes on, buddy.

420
00:18:33,733 --> 00:18:35,466
Let me show you what we got here.

421
00:18:41,733 --> 00:18:45,733
I was in Montana, and I put my car, uh...

422
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:47,666
Ran it off the road, put it in a pond.

423
00:18:47,733 --> 00:18:49,300
It's 7:00 in the morning,

424
00:18:49,666 --> 00:18:52,399
it's 15 degrees and it's snowing.

425
00:18:52,800 --> 00:18:55,333
Within ten minutes, a woman picks me up,

426
00:18:55,399 --> 00:18:57,433
takes me to a guy's house...

427
00:18:57,733 --> 00:18:59,899
-You're on your own in this car? Yeah.
-I'm on my own.

428
00:18:59,966 --> 00:19:03,000
And we're heading back toward the house,
and I see the guys coming.

429
00:19:03,066 --> 00:19:06,700
I get out, I get in with the guys,
they hook it up to a chain,

430
00:19:07,066 --> 00:19:10,333
they pop it out so that the rear end
is airborne.

431
00:19:10,533 --> 00:19:13,600
They just yanked it out.
Kaboom! Comes flying out.

432
00:19:13,652 --> 00:19:15,954
I said, "Thank you very much, boys."
I get back in the car.

433
00:19:16,133 --> 00:19:18,566
-It was tremendous.
-Wow! That's tremendous.

434
00:19:18,633 --> 00:19:21,866
Yes, it's just wonderful.
Now, if it had been me,

435
00:19:21,933 --> 00:19:23,399
they'd find me dead in the car.

436
00:19:23,466 --> 00:19:25,166
-Right. It's Misery.
-Yeah.

437
00:19:25,366 --> 00:19:27,533
[Jerry] You're meeting Kathy Bates
and getting hobbled.

438
00:19:27,766 --> 00:19:28,733
[David chuckling]

439
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:30,300
[Jerry] I love getting hobbled.

